        "content": "<p>Hi everyone, I'm Juan Pablo. I'm a physician by day, but as an independent researcher, I've spent the last while working on the 1/3-2/3 Poset Conjecture (Kislitsyn 1968, Fredman 1976).</p>\n<p>I've just finished a modular formalization in Lean 4 that reduces the combinatorial side of the conjecture down to a single geometric absolute: Stanley's Order Polytope volume-extension identity.</p>\n<p>The architecture relies on a \"Four-Bottleneck Dichotomy\" to isolate combinatorial failure points, proving the structural rigidity of the N-shape and Double Diamond cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Status of the repo:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><code>0</code> sorry statements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><code>1</code> axiom (<code>stanley_geometric_identity</code>).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Organized as a 4-module DAG.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I've pushed the code to GitHub along with a PDF detailing the proof architecture and the specific sub-cases.</p>\n<p>I am hitting the limit of my domain expertise when it comes to formalizing the continuous geometry and measure theory in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ required to integrate the order polytope volumes. I wanted to share this here to see if anyone interested in convex geometry or polytope volume bounds would like to collaborate to discharge this final topological axiom.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/ertwro/one_third_two_thirds\">https://github.com/ertwro/one_third_two_thirds</a><br>\n<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18985093\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18985093</a></p>\n<p>Looking forward to any feedback!</p>",
        "id": 578968777,
        "sender_full_name": "Juan Pablo Silva Alvarado",
        "timestamp": 1773332369
    },
