        "content": "<p>Repo upadated an paper updated: 03-15-2026<br>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/ertwro/one_third_two_thirds\">https://github.com/ertwro/one_third_two_thirds</a><br>\n<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18985093\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18985093</a></p>\n<p>Update: Beyond combinatorial reduction &amp; a quick apology</p>\n<p>Just wanted to drop a technical update on the 1/3-2/3 formalization, and I need to apologize.</p>\n<p>On the technical side: we are officially past the Stanley topology axiom. The combinatorial reduction is structurally complete for the lower bound <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\"> |S| ≤ 3 </annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∣</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.05764em;\">S</span><span class=\"mord\">∣</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span></span>. By keeping it strictly discrete and finite, the proof entirely bypasses the continuous geometry detour.</p>\n<p>On the apology side. I completely mistook what Lean is. When I started, I treated it like a normal compiler and just threw brute-force spaghetti code at it to force the <code>sorries</code> count to zero. I didn't realize until now that the integration, documentation, and beauty of the code are just as important as the proof itself. I feel pretty ashamed for missing the whole point of <code>mathlib</code> so badly in my early draft.</p>\n<p>Given the silence on my posts, part of me is terrified someone here actually tried to read the repo and went through hell trying to decipher the mess. But mostly I'm just relieved, hoping not many of you had to suffer through that Frankenstein code. I'm anxious and don't know if this is normal or not.</p>\n<p>I stopped trying to brute-force the final steps. Instead, I completely refactored the repo. I purged all the dead geometric paths, applied the maintainers' best practices, and wrote a proper formal translation dictionary to document the architecture before touching the code again.</p>\n<p>The repo is clean now. Am currently working on closing the final algebraic bound using this proper foundation.</p>",
